::ALG2_U03_L02_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L02_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of f(x) = &radic;(x - 7)? {
= x >= 7#Correct. The radicand x - 7 must be nonnegative.
~ x <= 7#That would make many radicands negative.
~ all real numbers#Square root functions require a nonnegative radicand.
~ y >= 7#Domain describes x-values, not y-values.
}

::ALG2_U03_L02_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L02_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the range of g(x) = &radic;x + 4? {
~ x >= 4#This confuses domain and range.
= y >= 4#Correct. The parent range y >= 0 shifts up 4.
~ y <= 4#The graph starts at 4 and increases.
~ all real numbers#A square root graph does not have all real y-values.
}

::ALG2_U03_L02_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L02_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the endpoint of h(x) = &radic;(x + 2) - 5? {
~ (2, -5)#The sign inside the radical is reversed.
~ (-5, -2)#The coordinates are reversed.
= (-2, -5)#Correct. The graph shifts left 2 and down 5.
~ (2, 5)#Both shifts have incorrect signs.
}

::ALG2_U03_L02_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L02_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which function is the parent square root function shifted right 3 units? {
~ f(x) = &radic;(x + 3)#Adding inside shifts left, not right.
~ f(x) = &radic;x + 3#Adding outside shifts up.
~ f(x) = &radic;x - 3#Subtracting outside shifts down.
= f(x) = &radic;(x - 3)#Correct. Subtracting inside shifts right 3.
}

::ALG2_U03_L02_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L02_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of f(x) = &radic;<sup>3</sup>(x + 1)? {
= all real numbers#Correct. Cube root functions accept negative, zero, and positive radicands.
~ x >= -1#That restriction applies to square roots, not cube roots.
~ x <= -1#Cube roots are defined on both sides of -1.
~ y >= 0#This describes a range restriction, not the domain.
}

